{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' report.md; sed -n '1,260p' scripts/group_analysis.py; cat input/upstream-methods.md; cat input/participants.tsv; cat input/sources.json; \\\"\"'$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" probes --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" fresh-rerun --help'","aggregated_output":".bashrc sourced.\n.bash_profile sourced\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\n## Finding\n\n**No whole-brain-corrected evidence supports a positive gain effect in vmPFC** in the NARPS equal-indifference group. The one-sided group test found positive corrected voxels, but they formed three small clusters in posterior regions (24 voxels total), not vmPFC. The strongest voxel was at MNI (−26, −102, −3.6), *t*(53) = 6.62, max-statistic FWE *p* = .0010. This result does not support the stated vmPFC hypothesis.\n\nThe NARPS analysis instructions required whole-brain-corrected binary decisions despite region-specific hypotheses; we therefore based the conclusion on whole-brain familywise error correction, not an uncorrected vmPFC search ([original instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf)). The vmPFC locator was a broad bilateral anterior midline/ventral MNI box (|x| ≤ 15, y = 25–60, z = −20–10 mm). Within it, the mean participant effect was −0.003 (95% *t* interval −0.021 to 0.016); its most positive voxel had max-statistic FWE *p* = .938. This coordinate box is an explicit operational locator because vmPFC has no single universally agreed boundary; its summary is descriptive and does not replace the whole-brain test.\n\n![All positive voxels surviving whole-brain FWE correction](figures/whole_brain_fwe_glassbrain.png)\n\n![Whole-brain corrected map shown at the vmPFC locator](figures/whole_brain_fwe_and_vmpfc.png)\n\n![Strongest corrected posterior cluster](figures/posterior_fwe_cluster.png)\n\n![Participant-level vmPFC effect estimates](figures/vmpfc_participant_effects.png)\n\n## Analysis\n\nWe analyzed all 54 supplied AO86 gain-effect maps in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space; no participants were excluded. Image shape, affine, and finite-valued data were checked. The whole-brain testing mask retained voxels with nonzero estimates in at least 90% of participants (210,968 voxels), reducing sensitivity to map-boundary coverage differences. We fit a one-sample *t* model across participant contrast estimates at each voxel. The directional alternative was evaluated with 5,000 participant-level sign flips; each permutation contributed its maximum positive *t* over the entire mask, controlling one-sided whole-brain FWE at α = .05. The 95th percentile maximum-*t* cutoff was 5.404. At this threshold there were 24 significant voxels in clusters of 17, 2, and 5 voxels, with peaks at (−26, −102, −3.6), (26, −98, −1.2), and (30, −94, −8.4) mm. A parametric one-sample *t* test with voxelwise Bonferroni correction also found 9 positive voxels, all in posterior regions.\n\nThe analysis ran with Python 3.13.16, NumPy 2.5.4, SciPy 1.18.1, Nilearn 0.14.1, NiBabel 5.4.2, pandas 3.0.6, and Matplotlib 3.11.2.\n\nThe maps are participant contrast parameter estimates, not standardized test statistics or group maps. Thus the group *t* statistics describe uncertainty across the 54 participants' supplied effects. The upstream gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution, so these coefficients cannot be read as a BOLD change per currency unit. They reflect gain modulation conditional on AO86's response-duration, choice, motion, censoring, drift, and separate gain/loss model choices. The preprocessing and individual models were treated as fixed, as instructed ([upstream methods](input/upstream-methods.md)); no time-series data were downloaded or re-modeled. The source cohort and image collection are documented in [the supplied provenance file](input/sources.json) and [AO86 NeuroVault collection](https://neurovault.org/collections/4932/).\n\n## Uncertainty and limits\n\nSign-flip maximum-*t* inference assumes independent participant effects that are exchangeable under sign reversal (in particular, a reasonably symmetric null distribution). The Monte Carlo correction used a fixed seed and 5,000 permutations, so its smallest attainable adjusted *p* is about .0002. The map-coverage threshold and broad coordinate box are analysis choices; the vmPFC interval is descriptive and not a separate confirmatory test. The upstream participant variance maps were not supplied, so group inference weights participant effects equally and cannot model their differing first-level precision. The result also inherits the fixed upstream model and the limits of its regressor scaling.\n\nFailure to find support means this analysis did not detect a positive gain-related effect in vmPFC at the whole-brain-corrected threshold. It does not establish that the true response is zero, rule out smaller effects, or address other regions, nonlinear gain responses, or alternative upstream models. The posterior corrected clusters do not rescue the vmPFC-specific claim.\n\n## Reproducible materials\n\n- [Analysis script](scripts/group_analysis.py) — validation, group inference, maps, tables, and figures.\n- [Analysis notebook](notebooks/narps_gain_group_analysis.ipynb) — run-through and result presentation.\n- `results/summary.json` — numerical summary, seed, and SHA-256 checksums for all input maps.\n- `results/fwe_clusters.csv` — corrected cluster coordinates and sizes.\n- `results/group_t_unthresholded.nii.gz`, `results/p_fwe_signflip_maxT.nii.gz`, and `results/group_t_fwe05_positive.nii.gz` — unthresholded *t*, adjusted *p*, and thresholded group maps.\n- `results/vmPFC_subject_means.csv` — participant-level ROI summary.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Group inference for NARPS AO86 equal-indifference gain cope maps.\n\nPrimary inference: one-sample positive t test with one-sided sign-flip\nmaximum-t familywise correction over a map-coverage mask. A parametric\none-sample t / Bonferroni result is saved as a sensitivity analysis.\n\"\"\"\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nimport hashlib\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport nibabel as nib\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom scipy.ndimage import label\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom nilearn import plotting, datasets\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nINPUT = ROOT / \"input\"\nOUT = ROOT / \"results\"\nFIG = ROOT / \"figures\"\nOUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nFIG.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nN_PERM = 5000\nSEED = 20261016\n\nparticipants = pd.read_csv(INPUT / \"participants.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\")\nfiles = sorted((INPUT / \"data\").glob(\"sub-*_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\"))\nexpected = set(participants.participant_id)\nfound = {f.name.split(\"_\")[0] for f in files}\nif found != expected or len(files) != 54:\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Map/cohort mismatch: {len(files)} maps, missing={expected-found}, extra={found-expected}\")\n\nimgs = [nib.load(str(f)) for f in files]\nref = imgs[0]\nfor f, im in zip(files, imgs):\n    if im.shape != ref.shape or not np.allclose(im.affine, ref.affine, atol=1e-5):\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"Spatial grid mismatch: {f.name}\")\ndata = np.stack([im.get_fdata(dtype=np.float32) for im in imgs])\nif not np.isfinite(data).all():\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Non-finite values found in supplied maps\")\n\n# Map coverage is inferred from nonzero estimates. Retain voxels nonzero in\n# at least 90% of maps, reducing boundary/coverage artifacts without\n# requiring an unprovided analysis mask.\ncoverage = np.count_nonzero(data, axis=0)\nmask = coverage >= int(np.ceil(0.90 * len(files)))\nX = data[:, mask].astype(np.float64, copy=False)\nn, nvox = X.shape\nmean = X.mean(axis=0)\nsd = X.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\nt_obs = mean / (sd / np.sqrt(n))\np_unc = stats.t.sf(t_obs, df=n - 1)\n\n# One-sided sign-flip maximum-t inference. Under the one-sample null, signs\n# are exchangeable when participant effects are symmetric about zero.\nrng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\nsum_sq = np.einsum(\"ij,ij->j\", X, X)\nmax_null = np.empty(N_PERM, dtype=np.float64)\nbatch = 50\nfor start in range(0, N_PERM, batch):\n    b = min(batch, N_PERM - start)\n    signs = rng.choice(np.array([-1.0, 1.0]), size=(b, n))\n    sums = signs @ X\n    denom = np.sqrt(np.maximum(n * sum_sq[None, :] - sums * sums, 1e-30))\n    t_perm = sums * np.sqrt(n - 1) / denom\n    max_null[start:start+b] = np.max(t_perm, axis=1)\n\nperm_cut = float(np.quantile(max_null, 0.95, method=\"higher\"))\np_fwe = (1 + N_PERM - np.searchsorted(np.sort(max_null), t_obs, side=\"left\")) / (N_PERM + 1)\nsig = p_fwe <= 0.05\n\n# Anatomical vmPFC approximation in MNI coordinates (bilateral medial,\n# anterior and ventral frontal cortex). It is used to localize/describe the\n# hypothesis region only; whole-brain corrected inference remains primary.\nijk = np.argwhere(mask)\nxyz = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, ijk)\nvm_roi = ((np.abs(xyz[:, 0]) <= 15) & (xyz[:, 1] >= 25) &\n          (xyz[:, 1] <= 60) & (xyz[:, 2] >= -20) & (xyz[:, 2] <= 10))\nroi_values = X[:, vm_roi]\nroi_subject_mean = roi_values.mean(axis=1)\nroi_mean = float(roi_subject_mean.mean())\nroi_se = float(stats.sem(roi_subject_mean))\nroi_ci = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n-1, loc=roi_mean, scale=roi_se)\nroi_t = float(roi_mean / roi_se)\nroi_p = float(stats.t.sf(roi_t, df=n-1))\nroi_vox = int(vm_roi.sum())\n\n# Write whole-brain images in the supplied spatial grid.\ndef full_img(values, fill=np.nan):\n    arr = np.full(mask.shape, fill, dtype=np.float32)\n    arr[mask] = values.astype(np.float32)\n    return nib.Nifti1Image(arr, ref.affine, ref.header)\n\nnib.save(full_img(t_obs), OUT / \"group_t_unthresholded.nii.gz\")\nnib.save(full_img(p_fwe), OUT / \"p_fwe_signflip_maxT.nii.gz\")\nnib.save(full_img(np.where(sig, t_obs, 0.0), fill=0), OUT / \"group_t_fwe05_positive.nii.gz\")\n\n# Parametric sensitivity: one-sided t probabilities with voxelwise Bonferroni.\np_bonf = np.minimum(1, p_unc * nvox)\nbonf_sig = p_bonf <= 0.05\n\n# Cluster summary of positive max-T significant voxels (26-neighbor).\nsig_img = np.zeros(mask.shape, dtype=np.uint8)\nsig_img[mask] = sig\nclusters, nclusters = label(sig_img, structure=np.ones((3, 3, 3), dtype=int))\ncluster_rows = []\nfor cid in range(1, nclusters + 1):\n    vox = np.argwhere(clusters == cid)\n    if len(vox) == 0:\n        continue\n    coord = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, vox)\n    vals = np.array([t_obs[np.flatnonzero(np.all(ijk == v, axis=1))[0]] for v in vox])\n    cluster_rows.append({\"cluster\": cid, \"voxels\": len(vox), \"peak_t\": float(vals.max()),\n                         \"peak_x\": float(coord[np.argmax(vals), 0]),\n                         \"peak_y\": float(coord[np.argmax(vals), 1]),\n                         \"peak_z\": float(coord[np.argmax(vals), 2]),\n                         \"centroid_x\": float(coord[:, 0].mean()),\n                         \"centroid_y\": float(coord[:, 1].mean()),\n                         \"centroid_z\": float(coord[:, 2].mean())})\npd.DataFrame(cluster_rows).to_csv(OUT / \"fwe_clusters.csv\", index=False)\n\nsummary = {\n    \"n_participants\": n, \"n_excluded\": 0, \"n_tested_voxels\": int(nvox),\n    \"mask_coverage_rule\": \"nonzero in >=90% of 54 maps\", \"n_permutations\": N_PERM,\n    \"random_seed\": SEED, \"permutation_maxT_95pct\": perm_cut,\n    \"n_voxels_fwe05_signflip\": int(sig.sum()),\n    \"n_voxels_bonferroni05_parametric\": int(bonf_sig.sum()),\n    \"peak_t\": float(t_obs.max()), \"peak_p_fwe_signflip\": float(p_fwe.min()),\n    \"vmPFC_roi_definition_mni\": {\"abs_x_max\": 15, \"y_min\": 25, \"y_max\": 60, \"z_min\": -20, \"z_max\": 10},\n    \"vmPFC_roi_voxels\": roi_vox, \"vmPFC_mean_effect\": roi_mean,\n    \"vmPFC_mean_effect_95ci\": [float(roi_ci[0]), float(roi_ci[1])],\n    \"vmPFC_t\": roi_t, \"vmPFC_one_sided_p_uncorrected_descriptive\": roi_p,\n    \"vmPFC_max_t\": float(t_obs[vm_roi].max()),\n    \"vmPFC_min_p_fwe_signflip\": float(p_fwe[vm_roi].min()),\n    \"vmPFC_min_p_bonferroni\": float(p_bonf[vm_roi].min()),\n    \"maps_shape\": list(ref.shape), \"voxel_size_mm\": [float(v) for v in ref.header.get_zooms()[:3]],\n    \"coordinate_system\": \"MNI152NLin2009cAsym (as supplied)\",\n    \"data_sha256\": {f.name: hashlib.sha256(f.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for f in files},\n}\n(OUT / \"summary.json\").write_text(json.dumps(summary, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\npd.DataFrame({\"participant_id\": [f.name.split(\"_\")[0] for f in files],\n              \"mean_vmPFC_effect\": roi_subject_mean}).to_csv(OUT / \"vmPFC_subject_means.csv\", index=False)\n\n# Compact scientific figures.\ntemplate = datasets.load_mni152_template(resolution=2)\nthreshold = perm_cut\ndisp = plotting.plot_stat_map(full_img(t_obs, fill=0), bg_img=template,\n    threshold=threshold, display_mode=\"ortho\", cut_coords=(0, 40, -5),\n    cmap=\"magma\", colorbar=True, black_bg=False,\n    title=\"Positive gain effect: sign-flip max-T FWE, p ≤ .05\")\ndisp.add_markers([(0, 40, -5)], marker_color=\"cyan\", marker_size=55)\ndisp.savefig(FIG / \"whole_brain_fwe_and_vmpfc.png\", dpi=180)\ndisp.close()\n\ndisp = plotting.plot_stat_map(full_img(t_obs, fill=0), bg_img=template,\n    threshold=threshold, display_mode=\"ortho\", cut_coords=(-26, -100, -4),\n    cmap=\"magma\", colorbar=True, black_bg=False,\n    title=\"Corrected posterior cluster at its peak (MNI −26, −102, −4)\")\ndisp.savefig(FIG / \"posterior_fwe_cluster.png\", dpi=180)\ndisp.close()\n\ndisp = plotting.plot_glass_brain(full_img(t_obs, fill=0), threshold=threshold,\n    display_mode=\"lyrz\", colorbar=True, cmap=\"magma\", black_bg=False,\n    title=\"All positive voxels surviving whole-brain FWE correction\")\ndisp.savefig(FIG / \"whole_brain_fwe_glassbrain.png\", dpi=180)\ndisp.close()\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4.2))\nax.scatter(np.arange(1, n+1), roi_subject_mean, color=\"#235789\", alpha=.8, s=28)\nax.axhline(0, color=\"black\", linewidth=1)\nax.axhline(roi_mean, color=\"#d1495b\", linewidth=2, label=f\"Mean = {roi_mean:.3f}\")\nax.fill_between([0, n+1], roi_ci[0], roi_ci[1], color=\"#d1495b\", alpha=.15,\n                label=\"95% t interval across participants\")\nax.set(xlim=(0, n+1), xlabel=\"Participant (ordered by ID)\",\n       ylabel=\"Mean gain-effect estimate in vmPFC box\",\n       title=\"Descriptive vmPFC summary (not whole-brain inference)\")\nax.legend(frameon=False)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig(FIG / \"vmpfc_participant_effects.png\", dpi=180)\nplt.close(fig)\n\nprint(json.dumps({k: v for k, v in summary.items() if k != \"data_sha256\"}, indent=2))\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\nparticipant_id\tgroup\tgender\tage\nsub-001\tequalIndifference\tM\t24\nsub-003\tequalIndifference\tF\t27\nsub-005\tequalIndifference\tM\t27\nsub-009\tequalIndifference\tM\t26\nsub-011\tequalIndifference\tM\t30\nsub-013\tequalIndifference\tM\t26\nsub-015\tequalIndifference\tF\t26\nsub-017\tequalIndifference\tF\t24\nsub-019\tequalIndifference\tM\t25\nsub-021\tequalIndifference\tF\t22\nsub-025\tequalIndifference\tM\t25\nsub-027\tequalIndifference\tF\t27\nsub-029\tequalIndifference\tM\t25\nsub-033\tequalIndifference\tM\t23\nsub-035\tequalIndifference\tM\t32\nsub-037\tequalIndifference\tF\t25\nsub-039\tequalIndifference\tF\t28\nsub-041\tequalIndifference\tF\t27\nsub-043\tequalIndifference\tM\t28\nsub-045\tequalIndifference\tF\t28\nsub-047\tequalIndifference\tM\t27\nsub-049\tequalIndifference\tF\t27\nsub-051\tequalIndifference\tF\t28\nsub-053\tequalIndifference\tF\t30\nsub-055\tequalIndifference\tF\t37\nsub-057\tequalIndifference\tF\t23\nsub-059\tequalIndifference\tM\t24\nsub-061\tequalIndifference\tF\t32\nsub-063\tequalIndifference\tF\t25\nsub-067\tequalIndifference\tF\t24\nsub-069\tequalIndifference\tF\t23\nsub-071\tequalIndifference\tF\t21\nsub-073\tequalIndifference\tM\t23\nsub-075\tequalIndifference\tF\t23\nsub-077\tequalIndifference\tM\t22\nsub-079\tequalIndifference\tF\t28\nsub-081\tequalIndifference\tF\t33\nsub-083\tequalIndifference\tM\t26\nsub-085\tequalIndifference\tM\t26\nsub-087\tequalIndifference\tF\t22\nsub-089\tequalIndifference\tM\t26\nsub-093\tequalIndifference\tF\t30\nsub-095\tequalIndifference\tM\t26\nsub-099\tequalIndifference\tM\t27\nsub-103\tequalIndifference\tF\t26\nsub-105\tequalIndifference\tF\t26\nsub-107\tequalIndifference\tM\t25\nsub-109\tequalIndifference\tM\t27\nsub-113\tequalIndifference\tF\t21\nsub-115\tequalIndifference\tM\t25\nsub-117\tequalIndifference\tF\t24\nsub-119\tequalIndifference\tF\t26\nsub-121\tequalIndifference\tF\t25\nsub-123\tequalIndifference\tM\t24\n{\n  \"study\": \"NARPS Hypothesis 1: group inference from fixed participant effects\",\n  \"collection\": \"https://neurovault.org/collections/4932/\",\n  \"team\": \"AO86\",\n  \"image_metadata_api\": \"https://neurovault.org/api/collections/4932/images/?limit=2000\",\n  \"selection\": \"All single-subject unthresholded hypo1 cope images; participant IDs exactly match the 54 equalIndifference participants in the pinned source table.\",\n  \"maps\": 54,\n  \"map_bytes\": 96387264,\n  \"map_type\": \"participant contrast parameter estimate, not a z/t statistic or a group result\",\n  \"spatial_reference\": \"MNI152NLin2009cAsym\",\n  \"upstream_processing_fixed\": true,\n  \"cohort_source\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenNeuroDatasets/ds001734/64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268/participants.tsv\",\n    \"git_commit\": \"64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268\",\n    \"sha256\": \"d127d700f9a519338da295ea56455bf7bb46ca572f726da72ef7b57c310687b8\",\n    \"transformation\": \"Select group == equalIndifference; preserve all columns and values.\"\n  },\n  \"methods_source\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/poldrack/narps/0a9d71a5ab435b40dcd50fea82d2961a43f0a3fc/ImageAnalyses/metadata_files/analysis_pipelines_for_analysis.xlsx\",\n    \"git_commit\": \"0a9d71a5ab435b40dcd50fea82d2961a43f0a3fc\",\n    \"sha256\": \"bfb66119c6beb26e5d3fc84638435cf6c49b40e5eb06606bfecaee0aa48d82b7\",\n    \"sheet\": \"Sheet1\",\n    \"header_excel_row\": 2,\n    \"team_id\": \"AO86\"\n  },\n  \"checksum_scope\": \"SHA256 values are computed from retrieved bytes; NeuroVault metadata independently supplies file sizes but no source checksums.\",\n  \"files\": {\n    \"data/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://neurovault.org/media/images/4932/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\",\n      \"image_id\": 118651,\n      \"participant_id\": \"sub-001\",\n      \"bytes\": 1794522,\n      \"sha256\": \"baf34